  "query_preview": "..\n\nA ridge of surface high pressure is providing an outstanding fall \nday across SE Mi as full sun counters slightly below normal \ntemperatures. Afternoon highs in the mid 50s to near 60 lead into \ndry and clear conditions with light wind this evening. \n\nAnother round of frost is expected tonight but with the Frost \nAdvisory focus on a target area mainly south of the Tri Cities and \nnorthern Thumb. Frost coverage is greatest toward the Ohio border \nand outside of metro Detroit, where the wind drops off under clear \nsky closer to the IN/OH surface high center. There is more of a \npressure gradient placed toward the Tri Cities and northern Thumb \nwhere SW wind holds in the 5 to 15 mph range. The wind is a \nconsiderable limiting factor for frost in that area late tonight and \nearly Sunday morning. \n\nThe SW wind toward the Tri Cities tonight strengthens across all \nareas during Sunday. This occurs within the increased pressure \ngradient between the Ohio valley surface ridge and the next cold \nfront extending into the upper Midwest from the large low pressure \nsystem near Hudson Bay. The start of a warming trend is the primary \noutcome for Lower Mi reflected in guidance Max T around 60 Sunday \nafternoon and in the mid 60s Monday. Dry weather is ensured through \nthe early week period as the Midwest front stalls and dissipates \nwell to our north through Tuesday. \n\nA split flow configuration in the larger scale mid/upper level \npattern places the Great Lakes in a favorable position to build on \nthe warming trend mid to late week. A progressive northern stream is \npositioned well north across Canada while a closed low dominates the \nCA coast into the 4 Corners. The mid level ridge scenario for the \nGreat Lakes has higher than normal predictability for dry weather \nand above normal temperatures that make a run toward 70 Thursday and \nFriday.",
